// Runtime/StringRuntime.maxon — the String value representation (P1.2 wave A) and the two builder-built
// Std runtime functions a String needs before ownership exists: `__print_string` and `__str_eq`.
//
// ⭐ A WAVE-A STRING IS IMMORTAL RDATA. A string LITERAL lowers to a pointer to a fused 48-byte record
// whose `buffer@0` points at a separate NUL-terminated byte blob — BOTH in read-only `.rdata`. Nothing
// is heap-allocated, refcounted or freed, so neither runtime function touches the MM runtime: a
// print-only or string-`==`-only program stays leak-gate-free (see `scanRuntimeUsage`). The owned heap
// String, its destructor, and drop-at-scope-exit are Wave B.
//
// This file owns TWO things and their single source is here:
//   1. the RECORD LAYOUT — the offsets `__print_string`/`__str_eq` read (`buffer@0`, `length@8`) AND
//      the offsets `LowerMaxonToStd` writes when it assembles a literal's record. One set of constants,
//      read from both ends, so the reader and the writer cannot drift (`buildStringRecordBytes` asserts
//      each field lands at its named offset).
//   2. the two runtime functions, built as `StdOp` graphs exactly as `MmRuntime` builds the allocator
//      — so they get register allocation, frames and the whole backend for free — reusing that file's
//      `ValueMinter`/`emitParams`/`oneI64Param`/`twoI64Params`/`argsOf` rather than a second copy.

// --- The fused 48-byte String record (bootstrap `MaxonToStandardConversion.Helpers.cs:503-536`) ---
//
// Every field is an 8-byte slot, so the six offsets are 0/8/16/24/32/40 and the record is 48 bytes.
// `buffer@0` is the ABSOLUTE VA of the byte blob — emitted as a PLACEHOLDER 0 and patched by the PE
// writer once `.rdata`'s RVA is known (a fixed-base image, so the VA is compile-time computable). The
// blob is a SEPARATE `.rdata` entry, NUL-terminated, and `length@8` EXCLUDES the NUL.
export let StringBufferOffset = 0 as ByteOffset
export let StringLengthOffset = 8 as ByteOffset

// Exported for `Parser.emitStringInterp`, which WRITES all six fields of an owned heap String's record
// (the literal path here only ever writes rdata). One set of offsets, read by the runtime and written by
// the interpolation lowering, so a reader and a writer of the same record cannot drift.
export let StringCapacityOffset = 16 as ByteOffset
export let StringElementSizeOffset = 24 as ByteOffset
export let StringParentPtrOffset = 32 as ByteOffset
export let StringIsAsciiOffset = 40 as ByteOffset
export let StringRecordBytes = 48 as ByteCount
let StringFieldBytes = 8 as ByteCount

// `element_size = 1` — a String's elements are bytes.
//
// ⭐ **A LITERAL'S `capacity@16`/`parent@32` ARE THE SHARED BUFFER-OWNERSHIP ENCODING'S** (`BufferOwnership`),
// not this file's invention and not the Array record's either: `RdataBufferCapacity` says the buffer is an
// immortal `.rdata` blob that is not this record's to write or to release, and `NoOwedAllocation` says the
// record owes no separate allocation a release. The Array record's byte-string literal writes exactly the
// same pair for exactly the same reason.
export let StringElementSize = 1 as ParsedInt

// ⭐ AN OWNED INTERPOLATION RESULT IS A HEAP STRING, and its bytes live INLINE — right after the 48-byte
// record, in the SAME `__mm_alloc` payload (`buffer@0 = record + StringRecordBytes`). So the whole thing
// is one allocation: `__mm_decref` → `__mm_free` reclaims the record AND its bytes together, and the
// destructor a struct passes (`TrivialDestructor`, 0) is exactly right — there is no external buffer to
// free. `capacity@16` is `InlineBufferCapacity` and `parent@32` is `NoOwedAllocation` (unwritten — the
// zeroing contract supplies it), which is the shared encoding saying exactly that: not mine to write in
// place, and no separate allocation owed. See `Parser.emitStringInterp`.
//
// The fixed bytes an owned interpolation result allocates BEYOND its text: the 48-byte record the bytes
// live inline behind, plus one trailing NUL (which the slab's zeroing supplies for free — nothing writes
// it). `Parser.emitStringInterp` sizes its one `__mm_alloc` as `totalLen + StringInterpOverheadBytes`.
export let StringInterpOverheadBytes = (StringRecordBytes as ParsedInt) + 1

// `isAsciiFlag@40` — 1 iff every byte is < 128 (a compile-time scan). An empty string is vacuously
// ASCII. The high-bit threshold and the two flag values are named rather than bare so the scan reads
// as the property it computes.
let AsciiHighBit = 128 as Byte
let AsciiFlagTrue = 1 as ParsedInt
let AsciiFlagFalse = 0 as ParsedInt

// The blob is the payload bytes followed by a single NUL — a C string, so the record's pointer is
// usable by any consumer that wants a NUL-terminated buffer, while `length@8` is what Maxon reads.
let NulTerminator = 0 as Byte

// ⭐ EVERY BLOB IS PADDED TO AN 8-BYTE MULTIPLE, so the RECORD that registers right after it lands at an
// 8-byte-aligned rdata offset. This is load-bearing for arm64-macOS ONLY: a String literal's `buffer@0`
// there is a dyld chained-fixup REBASE (`MachOWriter.writeMachoStringBufferRebases`), and a chain links
// its entries by a 4-byte-granular `next` delta, so every rebased slot MUST sit at an aligned offset or
// the delta cannot be encoded. Records are 48 bytes (an 8-multiple) and arm64 float islands are 8, so
// once blobs are 8-multiples the rdata cursor stays 8-aligned across the whole section and every record
// is naturally aligned. The trailing zero bytes are INVISIBLE to every consumer — the blob is read as a
// NUL-terminated C string from `buffer@0` and `length@8` counts only the payload — so this is free on
// x64 (which bakes an unaligned absolute VA and needs no alignment) and correct on arm64. `v1` does the
// same at its `.data` string helpers (LowerMaxonToStd `addSubprocessString`/`addTraceTagEntry`).
let StringBlobAlignment = 8 as ByteCount

// `__int_to_string`'s decimal machinery. The digits are rendered without ever NEGATING the value, so
// `i64.min` is handled with no overflow: signed `%`/`/` keep the value's sign (`x % 10 ∈ [-9,0]` for a
// negative `x`), and the per-digit magnitude is recovered by MULTIPLYING by `sign = 1 - 2*neg` (`+1` for
// a non-negative value, `-1` for a negative one) rather than by an `abs` that `i64.min` would overflow.
let AsciiZeroDigit = 48 as ParsedInt
let AsciiMinusSign = 45 as ParsedInt
let DecimalRadix = 10 as ParsedInt

// `value >>logical 63` is `1` exactly when the sign bit is set — the branch-free `isNegative` the sign
// and the leading `-` are both derived from.
let SignBitPosition = 63 as ParsedInt
let SignMultiplierBase = 1 as ParsedInt
let SignMultiplierSpan = 2 as ParsedInt

// The scratch an integer part reserves before `__int_to_string` fills it: `i64.min` renders as
// "-9223372036854775808", 20 digits and a sign = 21 bytes, and no i64 is wider. `Parser.emitStringInterp`
// allocates this, the converter fills `len ≤ 21` bytes, and the caller frees it after the copy.
export let IntToStringScratchBytes = 21 as ParsedInt

// `__bool_to_string` renders "true" (4) or "false" (5). The ASCII bytes of both, and the two lengths.
let AsciiLowerT = 116 as ParsedInt
let AsciiLowerR = 114 as ParsedInt
let AsciiLowerU = 117 as ParsedInt
let AsciiLowerE = 101 as ParsedInt
let AsciiLowerF = 102 as ParsedInt
let AsciiLowerA = 97 as ParsedInt
let AsciiLowerL = 108 as ParsedInt
let AsciiLowerS = 115 as ParsedInt
let BoolTrueLength = 4 as ParsedInt
let BoolFalseLength = 5 as ParsedInt

// "false" is the longest rendering, so 5 bytes cover both.
export let BoolToStringScratchBytes = 5 as ParsedInt

// The `.rdata` label prefixes for a literal's blob and its record. Distinct so a reader of the section
// can tell the two apart; `__`-prefixed so they never collide with a user name and stay out of the
// spec goldens (`TargetPrinter.isRuntimeFunction`'s data twin).
let StringBlobPrefix = "__str_blob_"
let StringRecordPrefix = "__str_rec_"

// The two runtime entry points this file installs. `__`-prefixed (`isCompilerInternalCallee` treats
// them as the compiler's own — no E3004, no signature slotting), but NOT `__mm_`-prefixed, so
// `isRuntimeCallee` leaves them out of the heap-runtime decision: a program that only prints or only
// compares strings carries neither the allocator nor the leak check.
export let PrintStringName = b"__print_string"
export let StrEqName = b"__str_eq"

// The two runtime entry points an interpolation lowers CALLS to (`Parser.emitStringInterp`). `__str_copy`
// blits one part's bytes into the result buffer; `__int_to_string` renders an integer part's decimal text
// into a scratch buffer. Both are `__`-prefixed compiler-internal (no E3004, no signature slotting) but NOT
// `__mm_`-prefixed — like `__print_string`/`__str_eq`, they touch no allocator, so they never widen the
// heap-runtime decision. The heap decision is driven by the interpolation's OWN `__mm_alloc` calls, which
// `scanRuntimeUsage` already sees.
export let StrCopyName = b"__str_copy"
export let IntToStringName = b"__int_to_string"
export let BoolToStringName = b"__bool_to_string"

// The two Wave-D entry points. `__str_append(self, other)` grows a String in place; `__str_decref(record)`
// is a String's drop — a `__mm_decref` that ALSO frees the external buffer of a detached String. Both are
// `__`-prefixed compiler-internal (no E3004, no signature slotting) but NOT `__mm_`-prefixed, so on their
// own they do not widen the heap decision. They DO call `__mm_alloc`/`__mm_free`/`__str_copy` inside their
// Std bodies — which `scanRuntimeUsage` cannot see, since those calls are not in the Maxon module — so a
// reference to either explicitly turns on `usesHeap` (and `__str_append` turns on `usesStrCopy`).
export let StrAppendName = b"__str_append"
export let StrDecrefName = b"__str_decref"

// ⭐ **WHERE A STRING RECORD KEEPS THE BUFFER-OWNERSHIP ENCODING** (`BufferOwnership`) — the three slots
// its predicates read, built from THIS file's offsets. All three provenances the record format admits are
// ordinary values of that encoding, and no String-only branch stands in front of it:
//
//   * INLINE (`capacity@16 = InlineBufferCapacity`, `parent@32 = NoOwedAllocation`) — every owned String
//     is born this way (`Parser.emitStringInterp`, which `promoteToOwnedString` also routes through). Not
//     mine to write in place, and no separate allocation owed: the record's own free takes the bytes.
//   * DETACHED (`capacity@16 >= 0`) — a separate `__mm_alloc`'d buffer this record owns and must release,
//     which is exactly what the encoding calls an OWNER, so its base is its own `buffer@0`.
//   * RDATA (`capacity@16 = RdataBufferCapacity`) — a literal's immortal blob. It never reaches these two
//     functions today: the parser promotes a `var` String at its declaration and only an owned String is
//     ever dropped. It needs no arm of its own regardless — it is a non-owned buffer owing nothing, and
//     both of those are already what the encoding says.
//
// ⚠ **THIS REPLACES `parent@32 == -1` ("a detached root"), AND THE BEHAVIOUR IT DECIDED IS UNCHANGED.**
// That test was this file's own third spelling of "is this buffer mine?" — and, at the same offset as the
// Array record's `parent@32`, it meant the OPPOSITE thing (there, *someone else's allocation I owe a
// release to*). Now `parent@32` means one thing in both records and nothing here has to be told which.
function stringBufferSlots() returns BufferRecordSlots
	return BufferRecordSlots.create(StringBufferOffset, capacityOffset: StringCapacityOffset, parentOffset: StringParentPtrOffset)
end 'stringBufferSlots'

// The bool `__str_eq` returns, as the i64 a Std `value(i64)` return carries: 1 when equal, 0 otherwise.
let StrEqEqual = 1 as ParsedInt
let StrEqNotEqual = 0 as ParsedInt

// True iff every byte is ASCII (< 128) — the `isAsciiFlag@40` scan, run once per distinct literal at
// lower time (the cold path), never per value.
export function bytesAreAscii(bytes ByteArray) returns bool
	for b in bytes 'each'
		if b >= AsciiHighBit 'high'
			return false
		end 'high'
	end 'each'
	return true
end 'bytesAreAscii'

// The NUL-terminated blob for a literal's payload — the bytes, then one NUL.
export function stringBlobBytes(bytes ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	var blob = ByteArray.create()
	blob.append(bytes)
	blob.push(NulTerminator)
	while blob.count() mod StringBlobAlignment != 0 'pad'
		blob.push(NulTerminator)
	end 'pad'
	return blob
end 'stringBlobBytes'

// Build the 48-byte record for a literal. `byteLength` EXCLUDES the NUL (it is the payload count);
// `isAscii` is `bytesAreAscii`'s answer.
//
// ⚠ **`buffer@0` HOLDS THE BLOB'S RDATA OFFSET, NOT 0, AND THIS IS LOAD-BEARING FOR DEDUP.** The PE
// writer OVERWRITES `buffer@0` with the blob's absolute VA (`ImageBase + rdataRva + blobOffset`), so
// the value stored here is irrelevant to what the program reads. But it is NOT irrelevant to
// `registerRdataConstant`, which DEDUPES records by their bytes: two different strings of the same
// length and ascii-ness have byte-identical records EXCEPT for `buffer@0`, so a placeholder of 0 would
// collapse `"hello"` and `"world"` into ONE record pointing at ONE blob — a silent wrong answer where
// every same-length string compares equal. Seeding `buffer@0` with the blob's own offset (distinct per
// distinct blob, shared by identical strings that already dedup their blob) makes records dedup EXACTLY
// when their strings are identical.
//
// ⭐ EACH FIELD IS APPENDED AT ITS NAMED OFFSET AND THE APPENDER ASSERTS IT LANDS THERE, so the offsets
// the runtime functions load by (`StringBufferOffset`, `StringLengthOffset`) and the offsets this
// writes are ONE set of constants that cannot drift: reorder a field or mis-size one and this panics
// rather than shipping a record the runtime reads wrong.
export function buildStringRecordBytes(blobOffset ByteOffset, byteLength ByteCount, isAscii bool) returns ByteArray
	var record = ByteArray.create()
	appendRecordField(record, expectedOffset: StringBufferOffset, value: blobOffset as ParsedInt)
	appendRecordField(record, expectedOffset: StringLengthOffset, value: byteLength as ParsedInt)
	appendRecordField(record, expectedOffset: StringCapacityOffset, value: RdataBufferCapacity)
	appendRecordField(record, expectedOffset: StringElementSizeOffset, value: StringElementSize)
	appendRecordField(record, expectedOffset: StringParentPtrOffset, value: NoOwedAllocation)
	appendRecordField(record, expectedOffset: StringIsAsciiOffset, value: asciiFlagValue(isAscii))

	if record.count() != StringRecordBytes 'sizeDrift'
		panic("buildStringRecordBytes: assembled {record.count()} bytes, not {StringRecordBytes} — a field's width does not match its offset spacing")
	end 'sizeDrift'
	return record
end 'buildStringRecordBytes'

function appendRecordField(record ByteArray, expectedOffset ByteOffset, value ParsedInt)
	if record.count() != expectedOffset 'offsetDrift'
		panic("buildStringRecordBytes: a record field is being written at offset {record.count()} but its constant says {expectedOffset} — the record layout the runtime reads and this writes have drifted")
	end 'offsetDrift'
	record.append(scalarImage(value, byteCount: StringFieldBytes))
end 'appendRecordField'

function asciiFlagValue(isAscii bool) returns ParsedInt
	if isAscii 'ascii'
		return AsciiFlagTrue
	end 'ascii'
	return AsciiFlagFalse
end 'asciiFlagValue'

// The `.rdata` labels a literal's blob and record register under. Split out so the two prefixes are
// named at the one place that uses them and `LowerMaxonToStd` names neither.
export function registerStringBlob(globalData GlobalDataTable, bytes ByteArray) returns String
	return globalData.registerRdataConstant(StringBlobPrefix, bytes: stringBlobBytes(bytes))
end 'registerStringBlob'

// Register a literal's record, seeded with its blob's offset (see `buildStringRecordBytes` for why the
// offset, not 0). `blobLabel` must already be registered — its offset is read back here.
export function registerStringRecord(globalData GlobalDataTable, blobLabel String, byteLength ByteCount, isAscii bool) returns String
	let blobOffset = globalData.rdataOffsetOf(blobLabel)
	return globalData.registerRdataConstant(StringRecordPrefix, bytes: buildStringRecordBytes(blobOffset, byteLength: byteLength, isAscii: isAscii))
end 'registerStringRecord'

// --- Which compiler-internal runtime functions a module references ---
//
// ⭐ ONE WALK, THREE ANSWERS. `moduleUsesHeap`'s job (does this program touch the heap?) and the two
// String questions (does it print? does it compare strings?) are the SAME traversal — every call op,
// once — so they are answered together rather than by three near-identical walks. Each answer keys a
// DIFFERENT installation, and the heap answer stays `__mm_`-specific: a print-only program must carry
// NO allocator and NO leak check, so `usesHeap` reads `isRuntimeCallee` (the `__mm_` prefix), never
// "any compiler-internal callee".
//
// It asks the MAXON module because the answers are needed BEFORE lowering — the heap decision drives
// `.data` layout, and all three drive which functions get installed into the Std module.

// The set of `__destruct_<U>` symbols a module CALLS. A SET, not a list, for the same reason
// `IrModule.dropFunctionsByName` interns its drop list: both `noteUnionDestructorUsage` and
// `installUnionDestructors` walk EVERY union (`project.enumTypes`) and ask "is this union's destructor
// referenced?" — a per-union membership test. Against a per-drop-site list that is an O(unions × drop
// sites) scan (the classic linear-lookup-in-a-linear-walk quadratic); against a set it is O(1) each, so
// the whole check is O(unions + drop sites). Insert also DEDUPLICATES — a union dropped D times records
// its one symbol once, not D copies.
export typealias DestructorSet = Set with ByteArray

export type RuntimeUsage
	export var usesHeap as bool
	// The program spawns a green thread (`async`/`await`). Drives `installGtRuntime` + the GT `.data`
	// globals + the two hand-assembled backend chunks. Set alongside `usesHeap` (spawn `__slab_alloc`s the
	// GT struct + arg buffer), from the `asyncCall`/`await` Maxon ops rather than a callee name — the async
	// ops are their own op kind, and their `__gt_*` calls do not exist until lowering.
	export var usesGt as bool
	export var usesPrint as bool
	export var usesStrEq as bool
	export var usesStrCopy as bool
	export var usesIntToString as bool
	export var usesBoolToString as bool
	export var usesStrAppend as bool
	export var usesStrDecref as bool
	// The program touches an `Array` (P1.7 slice 1). Any `__arr_*` runtime call turns this on — and, like
	// the String helpers, turns on `usesHeap` too, because the array helpers `__mm_alloc`/`__mm_free` the
	// backing record and its element buffer inside their Std bodies, which this Maxon-module scan cannot
	// see. It drives `installArrayRuntime` (all the array helpers, appended already-lowered).
	export var usesArray as bool
	// The program takes a SECOND owner of a heap box (`__mm_incref`). Two producers: the array runtime's
	// zero-copy slice (a view increfs the parent's element buffer — implied by `usesArray`, since the call
	// lives in a Std body this scan cannot see), and the PARSER's `try … otherwise` reconciliation, which
	// increfs an ok-edge borrow or an aliased error-edge binding so both merge into one owned phi — a `call
	// __mm_incref` this scan DOES see. Drives `installMmRuntime` to build `__mm_incref`; a program that never
	// increfs (the common one) keeps it out. Set alongside `usesHeap` (an incref is on a `__mm_alloc`'d box).
	export var usesIncref as bool
	// The program co-owns a BORROWED managed-aggregate parameter into a `var` (`var q = p`, OPEN #40),
	// which the parser makes a second owner of the caller's box via `__mm_retain`. Its OWN bit, not
	// `usesIncref`: `__mm_retain` (returns the pointer) and `__mm_incref` (void) have independent callers,
	// so folding either into the other installs a dead function in the program that uses only the other
	// (see `installMmRuntime`). A `call __mm_retain` this scan sees directly. Drives `installMmRuntime` to
	// build `__mm_retain`; a program that never co-owns a borrowed aggregate keeps it out. Set alongside
	// `usesHeap` (a retain is on a `__mm_alloc`'d box).
	export var usesRetain as bool
	// The `__destruct_<U>` symbols this module CALLS (P1.3 slice 2) — collected from the drop sites so
	// `installUnionDestructors` synthesizes only the referenced ones, and `noteUnionDestructorUsage`
	// can classify their transitive `__str_decref`/`__mm_free` needs. Full symbols (not names): the
	// consumers query them against `destructorSymbol(name)`, avoiding a byte-substring extract. A
	// SET so that per-union query is O(1) — see `DestructorSet`.
	export var neededDestructors as DestructorSet

	static function create() returns RuntimeUsage
		return Self{usesHeap: false, usesGt: false, usesPrint: false, usesStrEq: false, usesStrCopy: false, usesIntToString: false, usesBoolToString: false, usesStrAppend: false, usesStrDecref: false, usesArray: false, usesIncref: false, usesRetain: false, neededDestructors: DestructorSet.create()}
	end 'create'
end 'RuntimeUsage'

export function scanRuntimeUsage(module MaxonModule) returns RuntimeUsage
	var usage = RuntimeUsage.create()
	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
			let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("scanRuntimeUsage: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
			for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
				let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("scanRuntimeUsage: opIdx {opIdx} not in module.ops")
				classifyRuntimeCall(op, usage: usage)
			end 'eachOp'
		end 'eachBlock'
	end 'eachFunc'
	return usage
end 'scanRuntimeUsage'

function classifyRuntimeCall(op MaxonOp, usage RuntimeUsage)
	match op 'op'
		call(_, callee, _, _, _) then recordCallUsage(callee, usage: usage)
		literal to stringLiteral then break 'op'
		// A throwing call names a user callee whose runtime usage is scanned exactly as `call`'s is; the
		// throw/propagate terminators and `panicAbort` name no user callee (panic exits directly) (P1.4b).
		tryCall(_, _, callee, _, _, _) then recordCallUsage(callee, usage: usage)
		throwError to panicAbort then break 'op'
		// P1.5-A1: an `indirectCall` calls through a code POINTER and names no runtime callee, so it records
		// nothing. A `functionRef` naming a USER function is likewise a bare `lea` of no runtime consequence —
		// but one naming a COMPILER-INTERNAL callee (`__`-prefixed) is the address of an array element destructor
		// stamped into `element_destroy@40` (P1.7 slice 3), and THAT reference must install its target (a
		// `__str_decref`/`__destruct_<T>`/`__mm_decref`/`__arr_decref`) exactly as a direct `call` to it would —
		// its transitive needs then close through `noteDestructorUsage`. Recorded through the same `recordCallUsage`.
		functionRef(_, funcName) then recordFunctionRefUsage(funcName, usage: usage)
		indirectCall then break 'op'
		// P1.5-B1a/B2b: `async`/`await`/`try await` carry the green-thread scheduler. Detected by OP KIND
		// (their `__gt_*` calls do not exist until lowering), and imply the HEAP too — `__gt_spawn`
		// `__slab_alloc`s the GT struct and the argument buffer.
		asyncCall to tryAwait then recordGtUsage(usage)
		// `sizeof(T)` lowers to a plain `loadIndirect` of the layout descriptor — it names no runtime callee
		// and pulls in no runtime floor (the descriptor is pure `.rdata`), so it records no usage (P1.6-B1).
		sizeofType then break 'op'
		byteStringLiteral then recordByteStringUsage(usage)
		// The jump table names no runtime callee and pulls in no runtime floor — a bare indexed branch.
		switch then break 'op'
	end 'op'
end 'classifyRuntimeCall'

// An `async`/`await` op turns on both scheduler and heap: the scheduler runs the green threads, and the
// heap floor supplies the `__slab_alloc` that `__gt_spawn` and the async arg-buffer spill need.
function recordGtUsage(usage RuntimeUsage)
	usage.usesGt = true
	usage.usesHeap = true
end 'recordGtUsage'

// A `b"…"` byte-string literal turns on both the Array runtime and the heap under it — the same pair any
// `__arr_*` call declares (`recordCallUsage`'s array arm), because the literal IS an owned Array record
// (`__mm_alloc`) whose `.count()`/`.get()`/drop go through the array runtime.
function recordByteStringUsage(usage RuntimeUsage)
	usage.usesArray = true
	usage.usesHeap = true
end 'recordByteStringUsage'

// A `functionRef` whose target is a COMPILER-INTERNAL callee (`__`-prefixed) is an array element destructor's
// address stamped into `element_destroy@40` (P1.7 slice 3) — its target must be installed exactly as a direct
// `call` to it would, so it routes through the SAME `recordCallUsage` (turning on the heap/`usesStrDecref`/
// `usesArray` bit or adding to `neededDestructors`, per the callee). A `functionRef` to a USER function is a
// plain code-pointer `lea` of no runtime consequence and records nothing (the P1.5-A1 behaviour, unchanged).
function recordFunctionRefUsage(funcName ByteArray, usage RuntimeUsage)
	if isCompilerInternalCallee(funcName) 'runtimeAddress'
		recordCallUsage(funcName, usage: usage)
	end 'runtimeAddress'
end 'recordFunctionRefUsage'

// ⭐ **THE ONE CALLEE-NAME → RUNTIME-USAGE-BIT MAP.** Exported so `noteDestructorUsage` can root a
// managed opaque-`T` `Array` element's destructor (P1.7 slice 3b-iii) through the IDENTICAL mapping the
// module scan gives a callee named directly — a `__str_decref` turns on the String runtime, a `__arr_decref`
// the array runtime, a `__destruct_<T>` joins `neededDestructors` — so the reloc-baked destructor is
// installed for the writer to resolve exactly as if the module had called it.
export function recordCallUsage(callee ByteArray, usage RuntimeUsage)
	if isRuntimeCallee(callee) 'heap'
		usage.usesHeap = true
	end 'heap'
	// A DIRECT `__gt_*` runtime call carries the whole green-thread scheduler (P1.5 mid-body-yield). `async`/
	// `await`/`try await` are detected by op KIND above because their `__gt_*` calls do not exist until
	// lowering, but `sleep(ms)` lowers in the FRONT END to a `call __gt_sleep`, which IS a Maxon `call` this
	// scan sees — so any `__gt_*` callee here turns on the scheduler (and, via `recordGtUsage`, the heap).
	if isGtRuntimeCallee(callee) 'gt'
		recordGtUsage(usage)
	end 'gt'
	if callee.equals(PrintStringName) 'print'
		usage.usesPrint = true
	end 'print'
	if callee.equals(StrEqName) 'streq'
		usage.usesStrEq = true
	end 'streq'
	if callee.equals(StrCopyName) 'strcopy'
		usage.usesStrCopy = true
	end 'strcopy'
	if callee.equals(IntToStringName) 'inttostr'
		usage.usesIntToString = true
	end 'inttostr'
	if callee.equals(BoolToStringName) 'booltostr'
		usage.usesBoolToString = true
	end 'booltostr'
	// `__str_append` and `__str_decref` call `__mm_alloc`/`__mm_free` inside their Std bodies, which
	// this Maxon-module scan cannot see — so their TRANSITIVE heap dependency is declared here rather
	// than discovered. `__str_append` also blits with `__str_copy`. Turning these bits on is what
	// keeps a program that appends from missing its allocator or its copy loop.
	if callee.equals(StrAppendName) 'strappend'
		usage.usesStrAppend = true
		usage.usesHeap = true
		usage.usesStrCopy = true
	end 'strappend'
	if callee.equals(StrDecrefName) 'strdecref'
		usage.usesStrDecref = true
		usage.usesHeap = true
	end 'strdecref'
	// A `__mm_incref` the PARSER emitted (the `try … otherwise` phi reconciliation) — install the incref
	// helper for it, not only for the array runtime's `usesArray`. `usesHeap` follows because the box it
	// increfs was `__mm_alloc`'d (which set `usesHeap` at its own call site anyway).
	if callee.equals(MmIncrefName) 'incref'
		usage.usesIncref = true
		usage.usesHeap = true
	end 'incref'
	// A `__mm_retain` the PARSER emitted (co-owning a borrowed managed-aggregate parameter into a `var`,
	// OPEN #40) — install the retain helper for it. `usesHeap` follows because the box it retains was
	// `__mm_alloc`'d (which set `usesHeap` at that box's own allocation site anyway).
	if callee.equals(MmRetainName) 'retain'
		usage.usesRetain = true
		usage.usesHeap = true
	end 'retain'
	// Any `__arr_*` call carries the whole array runtime, and the heap under it: `__arr_create`/`__arr_grow`
	// `__mm_alloc` the record and the element buffer, `__arr_decref` `__mm_free`s them — none of which this
	// Maxon-module scan sees, since those calls live in the synthesized Std bodies. So a single reference
	// declares both bits, exactly as `__str_append`/`__str_decref` do for their own transitive heap use.
	if isArrayRuntimeCallee(callee) 'array'
		usage.usesArray = true
		usage.usesHeap = true
	end 'array'
	// A managed-payload union's scope-exit drop (`__destruct_<U>`). It frees the box (`__mm_free` — heap)
	// and, for a String field, calls `__str_decref` — but those live in the SYNTHESIZED body this scan
	// cannot see, so the symbol is recorded and its transitive needs are declared by
	// `noteUnionDestructorUsage` (which has the signatures/interner to classify the fields).
	if bytesStartWith(callee, prefix: DestructorPrefix) 'uniondtor'
		usage.usesHeap = true
		usage.neededDestructors.insert(callee)
	end 'uniondtor'
end 'recordCallUsage'

// Install the referenced String runtime functions into the lowered Std module. Called AFTER the
// pipeline (like `installMmRuntime`), so these are appended already-lowered and never re-optimized.
// Each is installed ONLY when referenced — vocabulary does not ship ahead of its consumer.
export function installStringRuntime(module StdModule, usage RuntimeUsage)
	if usage.usesPrint 'print'
		buildPrintString(module)
	end 'print'
	if usage.usesStrEq 'streq'
		buildStrEq(module)
	end 'streq'
	if usage.usesStrCopy 'strcopy'
		buildStrCopy(module)
	end 'strcopy'
	if usage.usesIntToString 'inttostr'
		buildIntToString(module)
	end 'inttostr'
	if usage.usesBoolToString 'booltostr'
		buildBoolToString(module)
	end 'booltostr'
	if usage.usesStrAppend 'strappend'
		buildStrAppend(module)
	end 'strappend'
	if usage.usesStrDecref 'strdecref'
		buildStrDecref(module)
	end 'strdecref'
end 'installStringRuntime'

// `__print_string(strPtr)` — write a String's bytes to stdout, no trailing newline.
//
// It loads `buffer@0` and `length@8` from the record and hands them to the `osWriteStdout` system op,
// which lowers per target — x64-windows to `GetStdHandle` + `WriteFile` through the IAT, arm64-macOS to
// a POSIX `write` through the GOT. The op is target-neutral, so this function names no OS symbol AND
// sets no frame size: `outgoingArgSize` (WriteFile's stack-arg space on x64, ZERO on arm64's all-register
// `write`) is a TARGET ABI fact, so each backend's isel sets it from the op it actually lowers
// (`StdToX64Conversion` bumps it for `osWriteStdout`; arm64 needs none). Setting it here would bake an
// x64 frame size into a target-neutral function — the leak that made the arm64 prologue panic.
function buildPrintString(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(PrintStringName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let strPtr = 0 as ValueId

	let buffer = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(buffer, addrId: strPtr, offset: StringBufferOffset, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let length = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(length, addrId: strPtr, offset: StringLengthOffset, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.osWriteStdout(buffer, lenId: length))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildPrintString'

// `__str_eq(a, b) -> i64(bool)` — content equality: equal LENGTHS and equal BYTES.
//
// This is the bootstrap's `bytesEqual` (a length check, then a byte loop), NOT the enum `memcmp`
// helper. It allocates nothing. Empty strings: length 0 == length 0 → the loop runs zero times →
// return 1.
//
// The byte loop carries its index `i` as a loop-header block-arg (a phi), entering as 0 from the
// length-match edge and as `i+1` from the back edge — the same on-the-fly SSA shape the parser mints
// for a `while`, here built directly because a runtime function has no parser. The header's phi
// `argType` is set to i64 explicitly: `retypeBlockArgs` runs in `lowerMaxonToStd`, which these
// post-pipeline functions never pass through.
function buildStrEq(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(StrEqName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var notEqual = module.addBlock(func, label: b"notEqual")
	var header = module.addBlock(func, label: b"header")
	var body = module.addBlock(func, label: b"body")
	var allMatch = module.addBlock(func, label: b"allMatch")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let a = 0 as ValueId
	let b = 1 as ValueId

	// entry: lengths differ ⇒ not equal; else fall into the loop with `i = 0`.
	let aLen = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(aLen, addrId: a, offset: StringLengthOffset, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let bLen = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(bLen, addrId: b, offset: StringLengthOffset, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let aBuf = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(aBuf, addrId: a, offset: StringBufferOffset, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let bBuf = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(bBuf, addrId: b, offset: StringBufferOffset, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let zero = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(zero, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let lenEq = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.cmp(lenEq, lhs: aLen, rhs: bLen, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(lenEq, thenBlockId: header.id, elseBlockId: notEqual.id))

	// The loop index `i` is the header's phi.
	let i = ids.mint()
	header.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(i, argType: StdType.i64))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(zero)))

	// header: `i >= len` ⇒ every byte matched.
	let atEnd = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(header, op: StdOp.cmp(atEnd, lhs: i, rhs: aLen, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(header, op: StdOp.condBranch(atEnd, thenBlockId: allMatch.id, elseBlockId: body.id))

	// body: compare `a[i]` vs `b[i]`; equal ⇒ continue with `i+1`, else not equal. The compare is the
	// LAST flag writer before the branch, so it fuses to a `jcc`.
	let aAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.binOp(aAddr, lhs: aBuf, rhs: i, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let aByte = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(aByte, addrId: aAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.u8))
	let bAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.binOp(bAddr, lhs: bBuf, rhs: i, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let bByte = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(bByte, addrId: bAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.u8))
	let one = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.const(one, value: 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let iNext = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.binOp(iNext, lhs: i, rhs: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let byteEq = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.cmp(byteEq, lhs: aByte, rhs: bByte, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(body, op: StdOp.condBranch(byteEq, thenBlockId: header.id, elseBlockId: notEqual.id))
	body.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(iNext)))

	let matchResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(allMatch, op: StdOp.const(matchResult, value: StrEqEqual, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(allMatch, op: StdOp.ret(matchResult))

	let mismatchResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(notEqual, op: StdOp.const(mismatchResult, value: StrEqNotEqual, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(notEqual, op: StdOp.ret(mismatchResult))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildStrEq'

// `__str_copy(dest, src, len)` — blit `len` bytes from `src` into `dest`. The one thing an interpolation
// does with every part: each part is materialized to a `(buffer, length)` pair and copied into the fused
// result buffer at a running cursor. A byte loop rather than a `rep movsb`/memcpy op because there is no
// memory-move op in the Std tier (`system` holds only `osAllocPages`/`osWriteStdout`), and a byte loop
// needs nothing the tier lacks. `len == 0` copies nothing (the header guard is `i >= len`, true at once).
//
// The loop index `i` is the header's phi — 0 from entry, `i+1` from the back edge — the same on-the-fly
// SSA shape `buildStrEq` builds directly because a runtime function has no parser.
function buildStrCopy(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(StrCopyName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 3, paramTypes: threeI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(3)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var header = module.addBlock(func, label: b"header")
	var body = module.addBlock(func, label: b"body")
	var done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 3)
	let dest = 0 as ValueId
	let src = 1 as ValueId
	let len = 2 as ValueId

	let zero = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(zero, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))

	let i = ids.mint()
	header.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(i, argType: StdType.i64))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(zero)))

	let atEnd = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(header, op: StdOp.cmp(atEnd, lhs: i, rhs: len, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(header, op: StdOp.condBranch(atEnd, thenBlockId: done.id, elseBlockId: body.id))

	let srcAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.binOp(srcAddr, lhs: src, rhs: i, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let srcByte = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(srcByte, addrId: srcAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.u8))
	let dstAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.binOp(dstAddr, lhs: dest, rhs: i, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(dstAddr, offset: 0, valueId: srcByte, valueType: StdType.u8))
	let one = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.const(one, value: 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let iNext = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.binOp(iNext, lhs: i, rhs: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(body, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))
	body.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(iNext)))

	module.setTerminator(done, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildStrCopy'

// `__int_to_string(value, dest) -> len` — render `value`'s signed decimal text into `dest`, returning the
// byte count written (never a NUL — the caller's buffer is zeroed and it copies exactly `len` bytes).
//
// ⭐ IT NEVER NEGATES, so `i64.min` needs no special case. `neg = value >>logical 63` is the sign bit as a
// 0/1. A leading `-` is written UNCONDITIONALLY at `dest[0]` and then OVERWRITTEN by the most-significant
// digit when the value is non-negative (that digit lands at `dest[start]` with `start = neg = 0`), so the
// sign costs no branch. Each digit is `value % 10` — which carries the value's sign — turned positive by
// MULTIPLYING by `sign = 1 - 2*neg` (`+1`/`-1`), again avoiding the `abs` that `i64.min` would overflow.
//
// Values dominating from `entry`/`setup` (`value`, `dest`, `neg`, `sign`) are read directly in the loop
// blocks those dominate; only the loop-variant `digits`/`v`/`pos` ride block-args. `len` is computed once
// in `writeSetup`, which dominates `done`.
function buildIntToString(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(IntToStringName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var zeroCase = module.addBlock(func, label: b"zeroCase")
	var setup = module.addBlock(func, label: b"setup")
	var countLoop = module.addBlock(func, label: b"countLoop")
	var writeSetup = module.addBlock(func, label: b"writeSetup")
	// The write loop is HEADER-GUARDED (test at the top), following `buildStrEq`/`buildStrCopy`'s shape so
	// the three string builders read the same way. It is a STYLE choice, not a correctness one. A fused
	// loop-test `cmp` whose boolean is a function's highest-numbered value once tripped the value-class
	// column — `setValueClass` sized it off the target OPERANDS, and a flags-only compare is not among them
	// — and this shape happened to dodge it by keeping the loop body's values minted last. That column now
	// GROWS to cover every DEFINED id (see `setValueClass` in StdToX64Conversion), so the dodge is
	// unnecessary and a bottom-tested loop would compile too; the shared shape stays for consistency.
	var writeHeader = module.addBlock(func, label: b"writeHeader")
	var writeBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"writeBody")
	var done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let value = 0 as ValueId
	let dest = 1 as ValueId

	// entry: `value == 0` prints a lone '0' (the count/write loops assume at least one non-zero step).
	let zeroConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(zeroConst, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let isZero = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.cmp(isZero, lhs: value, rhs: zeroConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(isZero, thenBlockId: zeroCase.id, elseBlockId: setup.id))

	let zeroDigitConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(zeroCase, op: StdOp.const(zeroDigitConst, value: AsciiZeroDigit, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(zeroCase, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(dest, offset: 0, valueId: zeroDigitConst, valueType: StdType.u8))
	let oneLen = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(zeroCase, op: StdOp.const(oneLen, value: 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(zeroCase, op: StdOp.ret(oneLen))

	// setup: derive `neg` (0/1) and `sign` (+1/-1), and write the provisional '-' at dest[0].
	let signBitConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(setup, op: StdOp.const(signBitConst, value: SignBitPosition, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let neg = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(setup, op: StdOp.binOp(neg, lhs: value, rhs: signBitConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shrLogical, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let minusConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(setup, op: StdOp.const(minusConst, value: AsciiMinusSign, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(setup, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(dest, offset: 0, valueId: minusConst, valueType: StdType.u8))
	let spanConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(setup, op: StdOp.const(spanConst, value: SignMultiplierSpan, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let twoNeg = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(setup, op: StdOp.binOp(twoNeg, lhs: spanConst, rhs: neg, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let signBaseConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(setup, op: StdOp.const(signBaseConst, value: SignMultiplierBase, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let sign = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(setup, op: StdOp.binOp(sign, lhs: signBaseConst, rhs: twoNeg, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let digitsZero = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(setup, op: StdOp.const(digitsZero, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(setup, op: StdOp.branch(countLoop.id))

	// countLoop(digits, v): count decimal digits — do { v = v/10; digits += 1 } while v != 0.
	let digits = ids.mint()
	let v = ids.mint()
	countLoop.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(digits, argType: StdType.i64))
	countLoop.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(v, argType: StdType.i64))
	setup.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(countLoop.id, argIds: twoArgs(digitsZero, second: value)))

	let radixCount = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(countLoop, op: StdOp.const(radixCount, value: DecimalRadix, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let vNext = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(countLoop, op: StdOp.div(vNext, lhs: v, rhs: radixCount))
	let oneCount = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(countLoop, op: StdOp.const(oneCount, value: 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let digitsNext = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(countLoop, op: StdOp.binOp(digitsNext, lhs: digits, rhs: oneCount, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let countZero = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(countLoop, op: StdOp.const(countZero, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let countDone = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(countLoop, op: StdOp.cmp(countDone, lhs: vNext, rhs: countZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(countLoop, op: StdOp.condBranch(countDone, thenBlockId: writeSetup.id, elseBlockId: countLoop.id))
	countLoop.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(countLoop.id, argIds: twoArgs(digitsNext, second: vNext)))

	// writeSetup: len = digits + neg; the last write position is len - 1. `digitsNext` is read straight —
	// `countLoop` defines it and dominates `writeSetup`, so the last iteration's count reaches here with no
	// exit phi (only the loop-carried `digits`/`v` recurrence needs a header phi). Then fall into the write
	// loop with the cursor at `lastPos` and the value to render.
	let len = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(writeSetup, op: StdOp.binOp(len, lhs: digitsNext, rhs: neg, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let oneWrite = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(writeSetup, op: StdOp.const(oneWrite, value: 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let lastPos = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(writeSetup, op: StdOp.binOp(lastPos, lhs: len, rhs: oneWrite, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(writeSetup, op: StdOp.branch(writeHeader.id))

	// writeHeader(pos, wv): stop when `wv == 0` — every digit has been consumed. The top-tested guard is
	// what keeps `writeBody`'s materialized values (not this `cmp`) the last minted; see the block header.
	let pos = ids.mint()
	let wv = ids.mint()
	writeHeader.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(pos, argType: StdType.i64))
	writeHeader.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(wv, argType: StdType.i64))
	writeSetup.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(writeHeader.id, argIds: twoArgs(lastPos, second: value)))

	let writeZero = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(writeHeader, op: StdOp.const(writeZero, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let writeDone = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(writeHeader, op: StdOp.cmp(writeDone, lhs: wv, rhs: writeZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(writeHeader, op: StdOp.condBranch(writeDone, thenBlockId: done.id, elseBlockId: writeBody.id))

	// writeBody: dest[pos] = '0' + (wv % 10)*sign, then wv /= 10, pos -= 1, and back to the header. `pos`
	// and `wv` are read straight from `writeHeader`, which dominates this block.
	let radixWrite = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(writeBody, op: StdOp.const(radixWrite, value: DecimalRadix, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let digit = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(writeBody, op: StdOp.mod(digit, lhs: wv, rhs: radixWrite))
	let digitAbs = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(writeBody, op: StdOp.binOp(digitAbs, lhs: digit, rhs: sign, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let asciiZero = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(writeBody, op: StdOp.const(asciiZero, value: AsciiZeroDigit, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let digitChar = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(writeBody, op: StdOp.binOp(digitChar, lhs: digitAbs, rhs: asciiZero, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let writeAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(writeBody, op: StdOp.binOp(writeAddr, lhs: dest, rhs: pos, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(writeBody, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(writeAddr, offset: 0, valueId: digitChar, valueType: StdType.u8))
	let wvRadix = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(writeBody, op: StdOp.const(wvRadix, value: DecimalRadix, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let wvNext = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(writeBody, op: StdOp.div(wvNext, lhs: wv, rhs: wvRadix))
	let oneWriteStep = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(writeBody, op: StdOp.const(oneWriteStep, value: 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let posNext = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(writeBody, op: StdOp.binOp(posNext, lhs: pos, rhs: oneWriteStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(writeBody, op: StdOp.branch(writeHeader.id))
	writeBody.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(writeHeader.id, argIds: twoArgs(posNext, second: wvNext)))

	module.setTerminator(done, op: StdOp.ret(len))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildIntToString'

// `__bool_to_string(value, dest) -> len` — render a bool as "true" (4 bytes) or "false" (5). The value is
// 0 or 1, so a single branch picks the rendering; each branch writes its bytes with fixed offsets and
// returns its length. The last value minted in each branch is the length const (returned, so materialized),
// never the entry `cmp`, so the value-class column is sized right (see `buildIntToString`'s header).
function buildBoolToString(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(BoolToStringName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var trueCase = module.addBlock(func, label: b"trueCase")
	var falseCase = module.addBlock(func, label: b"falseCase")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let value = 0 as ValueId
	let dest = 1 as ValueId

	let zeroConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(zeroConst, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let isFalse = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.cmp(isFalse, lhs: value, rhs: zeroConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(isFalse, thenBlockId: falseCase.id, elseBlockId: trueCase.id))

	storeConstByte(module, block: trueCase, ids: ids, dest: dest, offset: 0 as ByteOffset, charValue: AsciiLowerT)
	storeConstByte(module, block: trueCase, ids: ids, dest: dest, offset: 1 as ByteOffset, charValue: AsciiLowerR)
	storeConstByte(module, block: trueCase, ids: ids, dest: dest, offset: 2 as ByteOffset, charValue: AsciiLowerU)
	storeConstByte(module, block: trueCase, ids: ids, dest: dest, offset: 3 as ByteOffset, charValue: AsciiLowerE)
	let trueLen = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(trueCase, op: StdOp.const(trueLen, value: BoolTrueLength, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(trueCase, op: StdOp.ret(trueLen))

	storeConstByte(module, block: falseCase, ids: ids, dest: dest, offset: 0 as ByteOffset, charValue: AsciiLowerF)
	storeConstByte(module, block: falseCase, ids: ids, dest: dest, offset: 1 as ByteOffset, charValue: AsciiLowerA)
	storeConstByte(module, block: falseCase, ids: ids, dest: dest, offset: 2 as ByteOffset, charValue: AsciiLowerL)
	storeConstByte(module, block: falseCase, ids: ids, dest: dest, offset: 3 as ByteOffset, charValue: AsciiLowerS)
	storeConstByte(module, block: falseCase, ids: ids, dest: dest, offset: 4 as ByteOffset, charValue: AsciiLowerE)
	let falseLen = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(falseCase, op: StdOp.const(falseLen, value: BoolFalseLength, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(falseCase, op: StdOp.ret(falseLen))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildBoolToString'

// `__str_append(self, other)` — grow `self` in place by `other`'s bytes. The first time a shv2 String's
// bytes leave the inline/rdata region (Wave D), so the FIRST real exercise of the leak gate on a non-inline
// allocation. Adapted from v1's `__managed_mem_cow_check`+`grow` (`stdlib/Internals.maxon:3432-3491`) to
// shv2's fused record and static single-owner model (no refcount on the detach path).
//
// ⭐ **ONE TEST DECIDES THE DETACH — `emitBufferCannotHold`, WHICH IS THE SHARED ENCODING'S OWN, NOT A
// SECOND SPELLING OF "IS THIS BUFFER MINE?".** The bytes must move to a fresh external buffer whenever
// `capacity@16 < requiredLen`, and that single comparison covers the ordinary grow AND every non-owned
// buffer at once. WHY it covers the second is not stated here: it is a property of the capacity values,
// so it is written where they are declared (`BufferOwnership`), against a type that stops at `-1` so a
// provenance CANNOT be added that slips past this test. `requiredLen = selfLen + otherLen` is this
// function's half of that contract — a sum of two `length@8` byte counts, hence never negative.
//
// ⚠ **THE OLD ALLOCATION IS RELEASED THROUGH THE SHARED PAIR, AND ONLY AFTER BOTH COPIES ARE DONE.**
// `emitOwedAllocationBase` says WHICH allocation the record owes a release to before the detach republishes
// the fields that answer it (an inline String owes nothing — its bytes are in its own box), and
// `emitReleaseOwedBase` pays it, null-guarded, once the blit has finished reading. ⭐ The release rides a
// BLOCK ARG into `blit` (0 on the in-place edge) rather than happening in the detach block, and that is
// load-bearing rather than tidy: `s.append(s)` aliases `other` to `self`'s OLD buffer, so releasing before
// the blit is a read-after-release. It is invisible today — `__mm_free` reclaims nothing under the bump
// allocator (PLAN.md #108) — and it is a use-after-free the day it does.
//
// ⚠ **THE INVARIANT THIS RUNG USED TO SPELL AS `parent@32 == -1` IS UNCHANGED IN BEHAVIOUR**: a String
// that has detached owns an external buffer and must release it, and one whose bytes are inline or in
// `.rdata` must not. What changed is only WHERE that is written down — `capacity@16 >= 0` plus
// `emitOwedAllocationBase`, the same encoding the Array record reads — so a String and an Array can no longer
// disagree about what a record owns. Leave it wrong and the external buffer is never freed (a leak, exit
// 101) or an interior pointer is released (an over-release, also 101).
//
// Growth is EXACT (`requiredLen`): the specs pin the output, the byte length and the absence of a leak,
// none of which a geometric policy would change — and exact size keeps the alloc/free count trivially
// balanced. Geometric growth is a later optimization with no consumer here.
function buildStrAppend(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(StrAppendName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var inPlace = module.addBlock(func, label: b"inPlace")
	var detach = module.addBlock(func, label: b"detach")
	var baseJoin = module.addBlock(func, label: b"baseJoin")
	var blit = module.addBlock(func, label: b"blit")
	var clearAscii = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clearAscii")
	let done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let selfPtr = 0 as ValueId
	let otherPtr = 1 as ValueId

	// entry: load the fields every block reads (entry dominates all of them, so these need no phi),
	// compute the required length, and branch on whether the buffer already has room for it.
	let selfLen = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: selfPtr, offset: StringLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let selfBuf = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: selfPtr, offset: StringBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let otherLen = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: otherPtr, offset: StringLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let otherBuf = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: otherPtr, offset: StringBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let otherAscii = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: otherPtr, offset: StringIsAsciiOffset, ids: ids)
	let requiredLen = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: selfLen, b: otherLen, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let tooSmall = emitBufferCannotHold(module, block: entry, record: selfPtr, slots: stringBufferSlots(), requiredLen: requiredLen, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(tooSmall, thenBlockId: detach.id, elseBlockId: inPlace.id))

	// The allocation the blit owes a release to once it has finished reading: the pre-detach one, or none
	// at all when the bytes go into the buffer that is already there.
	let owedBase = ids.mint()
	blit.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(owedBase, argType: StdType.i64))

	let owesNothing = emitConst(module, block: inPlace, value: NoOwedAllocation, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(inPlace, op: StdOp.branch(blit.id))
	inPlace.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(blit.id, argIds: argsOf(owesNothing)))

	// detach: read what the record owes BEFORE the publish below overwrites the fields that answer it.
	let base = emitOwedAllocationBase(module, func: func, cur: detach, join: baseJoin, record: selfPtr, slots: stringBufferSlots(), ids: ids)

	// baseJoin: allocate a fresh external buffer (destructor 0 — raw bytes), copy the live bytes across,
	// and publish it. `parent@32` returns to `NoOwedAllocation`: a record that has just copied its bytes
	// into an allocation of its own owes nothing to anyone else, exactly as `__arr_cow_detach` leaves it.
	let zeroDtor = emitConst(module, block: baseJoin, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let newBuf = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(baseJoin, op: StdOp.call(newBuf, callee: MmAllocName, args: twoArgs(requiredLen, second: zeroDtor)))
	let copied = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(baseJoin, op: StdOp.call(copied, callee: StrCopyName, args: threeArgs(newBuf, second: selfBuf, third: selfLen)))
	emitStoreField(module, block: baseJoin, record: selfPtr, offset: StringBufferOffset, value: newBuf)
	emitStoreField(module, block: baseJoin, record: selfPtr, offset: StringCapacityOffset, value: requiredLen)
	let noParent = emitConst(module, block: baseJoin, value: NoOwedAllocation, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: baseJoin, record: selfPtr, offset: StringParentPtrOffset, value: noParent)
	module.setTerminator(baseJoin, op: StdOp.branch(blit.id))
	baseJoin.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(blit.id, argIds: argsOf(base)))

	// blit: append `other`'s bytes at the end of `self`'s CURRENT buffer (reloaded, since detach may have
	// replaced it) using the OLD length as the write cursor, then publish the new length. Reached in place
	// and grown alike. Only now is the old allocation released — see the aliasing note above.
	let curBuf = emitLoadField(module, block: blit, record: selfPtr, offset: StringBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let dstAddr = emitBin(module, block: blit, a: curBuf, b: selfLen, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let blitted = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(blit, op: StdOp.call(blitted, callee: StrCopyName, args: threeArgs(dstAddr, second: otherBuf, third: otherLen)))
	emitStoreField(module, block: blit, record: selfPtr, offset: StringLengthOffset, value: requiredLen)
	var afterRelease = emitReleaseOwedBase(module, func: func, cur: blit, base: owedBase, callee: MmDecrefName, ids: ids)

	// afterRelease: `isAscii` is maintained incrementally — the concatenation stays ASCII only if `other` is.
	let zeroAscii = emitConst(module, block: afterRelease, value: AsciiFlagFalse, ids: ids)
	let otherNonAscii = emitCmp(module, block: afterRelease, a: otherAscii, b: zeroAscii, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(afterRelease, op: StdOp.condBranch(otherNonAscii, thenBlockId: clearAscii.id, elseBlockId: done.id))

	// clearAscii: `other` carried a non-ASCII byte, so the result is not ASCII.
	emitStoreField(module, block: clearAscii, record: selfPtr, offset: StringIsAsciiOffset, value: zeroAscii)
	module.setTerminator(clearAscii, op: StdOp.branch(done.id))

	module.setTerminator(done, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildStrAppend'

// `__str_decref(record)` — a String's DROP. It is `__mm_decref` (dec the refcount; free at the last
// owner) plus the one thing the generic decref structurally cannot do: release the EXTERNAL buffer of a
// detached String BEFORE the record. The parser routes every owned-String drop here by TAG (`emitDecref`),
// so a struct box and a raw scratch buffer still drop through `__mm_decref`; only a String record, which
// alone carries the buffer-ownership encoding, drops through here.
//
// ⭐ **STATIC DISPATCH, NOT A DESTRUCTOR POINTER.** The record stores NO destructor — the parser knows the
// static type at every drop, so there is nothing to dispatch dynamically, and `funcAddr`/`callIndirect`
// stay deferred until a struct/array FIELD can hold a String (P1.4+), where a box's own `__mm_free` must
// reach a destructor it cannot name. Shipping the indirect-call vocabulary now would be vocabulary ahead
// of its forcing consumer — the mistake `__mm_incref` and `mintPhi` are the standing warnings against.
//
// The refcount read-modify-write is the shared `emitRefcountCheckToLastOwner` (load rc; `== 0` ⇒ last
// owner; else lower and keep) — the ONE home of the refcount protocol, so a String and a struct box
// cannot disagree about who the last owner is. `__str_decref` runs that shared check itself, rather
// than calling `__mm_decref` at runtime, because it must read `parent`/`buffer` out of the record on
// the last-owner path BEFORE that record is freed — the generic decref would reclaim the box first.
function buildStrDecref(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(StrDecrefName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var lower = module.addBlock(func, label: b"lower")
	var release = module.addBlock(func, label: b"release")
	var baseJoin = module.addBlock(func, label: b"baseJoin")
	var freeRec = module.addBlock(func, label: b"freeRec")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let record = 0 as ValueId

	// entry + lower: the shared refcount check. `rc == 0` (the last owner) branches to `release`, where a
	// String does the one thing the generic decref structurally cannot — release a detached buffer first.
	emitRefcountCheckToLastOwner(module, entry: entry, lower: lower, record: record, lastOwner: release, ids: ids)

	// release + baseJoin: the last owner asks the shared pair which separate allocation this record owes a
	// release to and pays it, BEFORE the record itself dies — the whole reason this cannot simply call
	// `__mm_decref`, which would reclaim the box while its `capacity`/`buffer` are still the answer. A
	// String that detached is an OWNER (`capacity@16 >= 0`) and owes its own `buffer@0`; one whose bytes are
	// inline (or in an immortal `.rdata` blob) has a negative capacity and leaves through the `parent@32`
	// arm holding `NoOwedAllocation`, which the null guard inside the release step turns into an ordinary
	// answer rather than a case. It is the SAME pair `__arr_decref` uses, so a String and an Array cannot
	// disagree about what a record owns.
	let base = emitOwedAllocationBase(module, func: func, cur: release, join: baseJoin, record: record, slots: stringBufferSlots(), ids: ids)
	var afterRelease = emitReleaseOwedBase(module, func: func, cur: baseJoin, base: base, callee: MmDecrefName, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(afterRelease, op: StdOp.branch(freeRec.id))

	// freeRec: release the record itself, its external bytes (if it had any) already released.
	let recFreed = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(freeRec, op: StdOp.call(recFreed, callee: MmFreeName, args: argsOf(record)))
	module.setTerminator(freeRec, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildStrDecref'

// Store one constant byte at `dest[offset]`. The shape a fixed-text renderer (`__bool_to_string`) writes
// each of its bytes with — a `const` typed i64, stored as a `u8` (the store keeps the low byte).
function storeConstByte(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, dest ValueId, offset ByteOffset, charValue ParsedInt)
	let ch = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.const(ch, value: charValue, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(dest, offset: offset, valueId: ch, valueType: StdType.u8))
end 'storeConstByte'

// `threeI64Params` — `__str_copy(dest, src, len)`'s three-i64 signature — lives in `MmRuntime` next to
// `oneI64Param`/`twoI64Params`, shared with the Array runtime (see MmRuntime).

// `twoArgs` / `threeArgs` — the pair and triple call/branch-edge builders — live in `MmRuntime` next
// to `argsOf`, shared by every runtime builder (see MmRuntime).
